@meta
id: 15-17
title: Scenario — the overnight incident
chapter: 15
type: scenario
skills: global-invert, global-delete, global-move
par: 72
minutes: 12
vscode: three tabs, a filter box, a lot of cutting and pasting
open: gateway.log payment.worker.ts registry.ts errors.json

@brief
A ticket, not an exercise.

    INC-207  Payment gateway timed out from 00:01 to 00:03. The worker
             was hot-patched during the incident. Clean up before the
             postmortem at 11:00.

Four files are open. Do them in order; `:w` each one before `:bn` moves
you to the next, and finish with `:wqa`. `:wq` will refuse with more
files open.

  1. gateway.log — the postmortem wants only the failures. Keep every
     `ERROR` line and nothing else.

  2. payment.worker.ts — whoever hot-patched it left `console.log` calls
     everywhere. All of them go. The `logger` calls are real and stay.

  3. registry.ts — the incident fix registered `RetryProvider` and
     `RetryConsumer` in a hurry, each `import` sat on top of its
     `container.register`. Move every `container.register` line to the
     bottom of the file, keeping their current order. The imports stay.

  4. errors.json — open so you can check the codes the worker throws
     exist. It does not change. Change it and the check fails.

Every file is one `:g` or `:v`. If you find yourself pressing `n` or
`dd`, stop and name the lines instead.

@keys
| `:v/pattern/d` | keep only the lines that match |
| `:g/pattern/d` | delete every line that matches |
| `:g/pattern/m$` | move every match to the bottom, in order |
| `:w` then `:bn` | write this file, then go to the next buffer |
| `:wqa` | write everything and quit |

@hints
The log is a keep, not a delete. Name what stays.
--
The worker's pattern needs to be `console`, not `log`: `logger.info` has
`log` in it too, and those lines are wanted.
--
The registry is the same move as lesson 12. `m$` keeps order; `m0` would
reverse it.
--
`:v/ERROR/d`, `:w`, `:bn`, `:g/console/d`, `:w`, `:bn`,
`:g/^container/m$`, `:wqa`. Do not open `errors.json` at all; it is
there to be left alone.

@solution
**gateway.log** — `:v/ERROR/d`, then `:w` and `:bn`.

**payment.worker.ts** — `:g/console/d`, then `:w` and `:bn`.

**registry.ts** — `:g/^container/m$`, then `:wqa`.

**errors.json** — nothing.

Three commands and three files, and no line was ever selected by hand.
Everything in this scenario was in the first twelve lessons of the
chapter; what it tests is whether you reach for the pattern before you
reach for `j`.

The judgement calls, for the record. The log was a `:v` because the keep
set has one name and the drop set has three. The worker was a `:g//d`
with a pattern chosen against the file, not against the word: `log`
alone would have matched `logger`, and you would have found out by
reading the diff. The registry was `m$` and not `m0` because order was
part of the ticket. Reading the ticket for the words that constrain the
tool is most of the skill.

